// Werewolf: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== werewolf.f01 | feature | frame
title: The Hound Of God
genre: werewolf + courtroom-drama
tone: Wintry, sardonic and strange
era: 1692 | period
setting: A manor court in Swedish Livonia | fictional | court
logline: An eighty-year-old peasant called as a witness in a petty theft cheerfully tells the court that he is a werewolf, and that three nights a year he goes down to hell to fight the devil for the harvest.
premise: The judges want to know who stole from the church. {thiess} is only there to give evidence, but everyone knows what he is and he sees no reason to deny it. He does not eat children. He is, he explains, one of God's dogs: the sorcerers carry the grain blossom down to hell, and the werewolves go after them with iron whips and bring it back, or there is famine.
synopsis: The frame is a courtroom in winter and three judges who would like to be home. The first act is disbelief. {thiess} is old, poor, well liked and entirely matter-of-fact; his nose was broken, he says, by a sorcerer with a broom handle, down there. The presiding judge {von_palmberg} tries to reclassify him as mad. The pastor {buhrmann} wants him to say that this is the devil's work. {thiess} is offended: he hates the devil; he has fought him for sixty years; who do they think has kept their barley? The second act goes where the testimony goes. The film shows his account without deciding it: a midwinter river, a company of men and women hanging their clothes on a thorn, a descent through marsh ice to a country of stored seed and firelight, a battle of dogs and broomsticks that is frightening and comic and oddly domestic. Did they not have a bad harvest the year he was ill? They did. A young notary, {gertrud_son}, the only local on the bench, is the only one who understands that the old man is not confessing but reporting for duty. The third act is the court's problem. He has harmed nobody. He will not repent, because he has done nothing wrong, and he will not stop, because then the sorcerers win. He heals cattle with salt and a blessing in which God is named and the devil is not. There is no law for a werewolf on the right side. They sentence him to a flogging for superstition. He takes them, thanks the court, and asks if he may go, since it is nearly St. Lucy's night. The last images are a thorn bush with an old man's coat on it, and in the summer a field of barley the judges ride past without comment.
question: What do you do with a heresy that is on your side?
themes: belief below the official religion; the comedy of jurisdiction; who actually guards the harvest
audience: Adults who like folklore taken seriously
viewpoint: The court's, and then his; the film never rules between them
stakes: An old man's back, a pastor's authority, and a district's barley, if he is right
conflict: A court that needs a devil-worshipper or a madman against a witness who insists on being neither
ending: He is flogged for superstition and goes out to do it again. The harvest is good.
experience: Laughter that turns to wonder, a visit to a very peculiar hell, and a field at the end that might mean something
look: Snow light in a panelled room; a night river; the underworld as a peasant barn, vast, lit by seed-coloured fire
sound: A stove ticking, quills. Under the testimony, dogs. A whip that rings like iron.
m: protect | institution | faith-vs-evidence | ironic
@ thiess | Old Thiess | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 82 | To be let go by St. Lucy's night | The blossom has to be fetched back | A court that wants him to be something else | Cheerful, stubborn, devout in his own way | Unchanged; the court changes round him | Plain Latvian peasant speech, full of practical detail | Patched coat, a nose broken sideways
@ von_palmberg | Judge Bengt von Palmberg | co-lead | antagonist | human | he/him | 50 | A sentence the appeals court will uphold | He is a rational Swede administering peasants | No statute fits | Fair, impatient, curious against his will | From "is the man mad" to a lenient sentence he cannot explain | Dry, Latinate | Fur-lined robe, cold hands
@ buhrmann | Pastor Magnus Buhrmann | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 44 | Repentance | The parish goes to this old man before it comes to him | He will not say the devil taught him | Earnest, anxious, outmatched | From exhortation to silence | Sermon cadence | Black gown, white bands
@ gertrud_son | Notary Jekabs Gertrudsons | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 27 | To write down what was actually said | His grandmother knew the same stories | He sits on the wrong side of the table for his sympathies | Quiet, bilingual, observant | From scribe to the one who asks the right question | Careful German; Latvian asides to the witness | Plain coat, ink-stained fingers
~ thiess - von_palmberg | witness and judge | a nuisance | a puzzle sentenced as lightly as the law allows
~ thiess - buhrmann | parishioner and pastor | a demand to repent | an old man pitying a young one
~ thiess - gertrud_son | witness and notary | a translator | the only one who understood
